```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The root cause is that router16's OSPF process configuration is not persisted to disk.
The file /etc/frr/ospfd.conf is 0 bytes (created empty at container start on Aug 15
01:05); the two critical network statements — `network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0` and
`network 172.30.1.0/24 area 0` — exist only in ospfd's runtime memory, applied once
at lab startup via the Kathara startup script but never written back with `write memory`.

When ospfd crashes for any reason, watchfrr automatically restarts it. Because ospfd.conf
is empty, ospfd comes back with no OSPF configuration at all: it forms no adjacencies,
learns no external routes, and advertises nothing. Router16 is campus 1's sole uplink
router (mswitch0 connects to the outside world exclusively through router16 → router7).
With router16's OSPF dark, mswitch0 loses every route beyond the 10.2.2.0/24 link
segment, the campus-1 VLAN prefixes (172.16.10–30.0/24) stop being advertised into
the OSPF domain, and campus 2 (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24 served by sshswitch/router17)
becomes completely unreachable from every campus-1 host — matching the described symptom
exactly. The outage persists until the entire router16 container is restarted (re-running
the startup script), explaining why the problem recurs every time ospfd drops.

At the moment of this investigation OSPF had already reconverged (~13 minutes of stable
Full adjacency with both mswitch0 and router7); connectivity is restored.

Corrective fix: in router16's Kathara .startup file, add `vtysh -c "write memory"` as
the final step after applying OSPF configuration. This populates /etc/frr/ospfd.conf
so that ospfd can reload a working configuration autonomously on any future watchfrr-
triggered restart, without requiring a full container reboot.
=== END ===
```